6. ISL RECOGNITION ENGINE
=========================

Build a modular recognition architecture.

Pipeline:

Webcam
-> Frame acquisition
-> Hand landmark detection
-> Landmark normalization
-> Feature extraction
-> Custom classifier
-> Prediction smoothing
-> Confidence and uncertainty handling
-> Verified vocabulary mapping
-> Text output
-> Optional speech output

STATIC SIGNS

For the initial MVP:

* Use normalized 21-point hand landmarks.
* Represent one hand as 21 x 3 coordinates when all coordinates are available.
* Normalize coordinates relative to the wrist and hand scale.
* Handle missing hands.
* Support left/right-hand variation where appropriate.
* Do not assume that mirroring the image preserves every sign's meaning.
* Do not treat every frame as an independent user message.

MODEL INTERFACE

Create a common interface similar to:

recognize(features) -> {
label,
confidence,
modelVersion,
status
}

Possible statuses:

* recognized
* uncertain
* unknown
* model_unavailable

Keep landmark extraction separate from classification.

The first implementation may include:

1. Landmark detection.
2. A recognition interface.
3. A small explicitly labeled demonstration classifier, only if it is genuinely implemented.
4. A custom-model loading mechanism.
5. Clear instructions for adding trained weights.

A demonstration classifier must never claim to recognize genuine ISL unless it has been trained and evaluated using verified ISL samples.

CUSTOM TRAINED MODEL

Design the frontend so a compatible trained model can be loaded later.

For browser inference:

* Prefer a compatible TensorFlow.js model or a documented ONNX Runtime Web model where appropriate.
* Verify model input and output shapes.
* Validate the vocabulary label mapping.
* Validate model version and metadata.
* Handle model loading failures.
* Do not load arbitrary executable code from an imported dataset.

For a Python-based training option, create a separate training directory and document:

* Dataset structure.
* Landmark extraction.
* Feature normalization.
* Train/validation/test split.
* Classifier training.
* Evaluation.
* Export format.
* Conversion or serving requirements for browser integration.

Do not promise that a Python pickle or joblib file can be imported and executed directly in a React browser application.

